## The nine quips, shared by all ten stallkeepers. `ST1`-`ST9` in file order.

define phrase st-opener
  'What do you want?' snaps the stallkeeper.
end phrase

define phrase st-for-sale
  'So, what are you selling today?' you ask, trying to sound casual.

  The merchant sneers at you. 'You have any coin?'
end phrase

define phrase st-mercenaries
  'You know anything about these mercenaries running around Grubber's?' you ask.

  'Only that they're bad for business,' mutters the stallkeeper. 'I don't know
  who hired them, but the Lord's Guard ought to run them off.'
end phrase

define phrase st-buy
  'I'd like one of those, please,' you say, pointing.

  The merchant sneers at you. 'You have any coin?'
end phrase

define phrase st-coin-yes
  'Yes,' you respond indignantly, 'and lots of it.'

  The stallkeeper snorts derisively. 'If you've half a penny that isn't stolen
  from somewhere, I'll sell my stall to the monkey. Now go bother someone else
  before I call those mercenaries over here myself!'
end phrase

define phrase st-coin-no
  'Um, no,' you say.

  The stallkeeper rolls his eyes. 'Do I look like I have time to gab at you all
  day? Beat it, kid.'
end phrase

define phrase st-nevermind
  'Uh, never mind.'

  'Whatever,' the stallkeeper mutters, and goes back to nervously scanning the
  crowd.
end phrase

define phrase st-goodbye
  'Yeah, fine, scram,' the stallkeeper mutters.
end phrase

## The patience counter. The third-approach line is Gentry's `[one of]beat it
## [or]scram[or]get out of here[at random]`, expanded to three variants because
## Chord randomises whole phrase arms rather than words inside one.

define phrase st-patience-second
  The stallkeeper suddenly looks exasperated. 'Didn't I tell you to take it
  somewhere else?'
end phrase

define phrase st-patience-third, randomly
  'I said, beat it!' the stallkeeper yells.
or
  'I said, scram!' the stallkeeper yells.
or
  'I said, get out of here!' the stallkeeper yells.
end phrase

